<sect>gridop (Revision 1.10 20/05/1999)
<p>
<sect1>Type definition of GGL_grid
<p>

<sect2>Macro SURFER_MASK
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
 #define SURFERMASK (1.70141e38)
 #define SURFERMASK_STR " 1.70141e38"
</verb></tscreen>
This value is used in Surfer Grids say, that there is no valid data.

<sect2>struct Grid
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct GGL_grid {
  char *grdfilename;      /* name of grid file */
  FILE *fp;               /* file pointer to gridfile */
  char *id;               /* identification string */
  long int nx, ny;        /* number of columns and rows */
  double xmin, xmax;      /* x-range */
  double ymin, ymax;      /* y-range */
  double zmin, zmax;      /* z-range */
  double dx, dy;          /* spacing in x and y direction */
  
  double offset, scale;   /* zvalue = offset + data*scale */
  double mask;            /* mask value */

  char datatype;          /* u = unsigned char */
                          /* c = char */
                          /* d = unsigned short int */
                          /* i = short int */
                          /* f = float */
                          /* e = double */      

  unsigned char datasize; /* size of corresponding datatype */
  long int d_offset;      /* offset to first element */
  long int d_row;         /* length of row */
  void *data;             /* pointer to data buffer */
}
</verb></tscreen>
The structure <tt/Grid/ contains header information and
either a pointer to the grid data stored in the internal 
memory <tt/data/ or a file pointer to an external raster
file. If not specified differently function returning
an integer, return 0 on successful completion.
<p>

<sect1> General Grid Operations
<p>

<sect2>g_init0
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Grid * g_init0()
</verb></tscreen>
<p> initialize basic griddata structure without memory allocation for data 

<sect2>g_getmem4data
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void * g_getmem4data(struct Grid * g)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> initialize memory for griddata depending on datatype. 
The necessary information is taken from the header info
of the grid itself. 

<sect2>g_free
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void g_free(struct Grid * g)
</verb></tscreen>
frees the allocated memory.

<sect2>g_index
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
long int g_index(struct Grid *g, const long int ix, const long int iy)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> returns offset in bytes for grid point (ix, iy)  

<sect2>g_printinfo
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void g_printinfo(FILE *fp, struct Grid *g)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> writes grid info to file (especially useful for debugging purposes)

<sect2>g_scalelimits
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void g_scalelimits(struct Grid *g, double xscale, double yscale)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> scales limits <tt/xmin xmax dx/ by a factor <tt/xscale/ 
and  <tt/ymin ymax dy/ by a factor of <tt/yscale/.


<sect2>g_getval, get_value, g_putval, g_putvalue
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
double g_getval  (struct Grid *g, long int offset)
int    g_putval  (struct Grid *g, long int offset, const double val);
double g_getvalue(struct Grid *g, const long int ix, const long int iy)
int    g_putvalue(struct Grid *g, const long int ix, const long int iy, const double v);
</verb></tscreen>
<p> <tt/get/ returns the value as a double. <tt/put/ puts the value <tt/v/.
<p>
Note: <tt/getvalue(g,i,j) == getval(g, g_index(g,i,j))/

<sect2>g_minmax
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void g_minmax(struct Grid *g)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> updates <tt/zmin/ and <tt/zmax/ based on the actual griddata.

<sect2>g_getcorner
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int g_getcorner(struct Grid *g, const double x, const double y, 
                double z[4], double * x0, double *y0)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> returns the 4 corner values <tt/z/ surrounding the point <tt/P(x,y)/.
In addition the coordinates <tt/x0/ and <tt/y0/ of the lower left corner (P0)
are returned. The ordering of the points is defined by:
<verb>
     y
     |
     P3 - P2
     |  P |
     P0 - P1  -> x
</verb>


<sect2>g_getpoint
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int g_getpoint (struct Grid *g, const long int ix, const long int iy, double xyz[3])
</verb></tscreen>
<p>returns  a vector xyz[3] with x-, y-, and z-values from grid at position (ix,iy).

<sect2>g_inter_simple
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int g_inter_simple (struct Grid *g, const double x, const double y, double *zvalue)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>  returns interpolated value <tt/zvalue/ for point with coordinates <tt/(x, y)/ 
calculated from the weighted average over the 4 corner points (weight = 1/sqr(distance)).

<sect2>g_inter_bilinear 
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int g_inter_bilinear (struct Grid *g, const double x, const double y, double *zvalue)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> returns interpolated value <tt/zvalue/ for point with coordinates <tt/(x, y)/ 
calculated from a bilinear interpolation.

<sect2>next_substring
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
char * next_substring (char *line, int *n)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This function returns a pointer to the start of next substring and
its length in *n. The substring can be delimited either by Space, " or \.

###### GMT operations ######

<sect1>Operations on GMT rasterfiles
<p> The following 2 environments variables are used : 
<p>
<tt/GRIDDIR/ = path to directory with rasterfiles.<newline>
(example.:/mnt/molnya/linux/gmt3.0/grids).
<p><tt/RASTERINFO/ = filename of GMT info file concerning rasters.<newline>
(example :/mnt/molnya/linux/gmt3.0/grids/grdraster.info)

<sect2> R_option, I_option
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int R_option( char * s, double *xmin, double *xmax, double *ymin, double *ymax)
int I_option( char * s, double *dx, double *dy);
</verb></tscreen>
<p>decodes a R-option or I-option string resp. Example: -R0/360/-90/90 -I15m.


<sect2>gmt_rasterinfo
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Grid * gmt_rasterinfo(int grdnr)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> opens RASTERINFO, initializes new grid, reads info corresponding to grid with number
<tt/grdnr/ (See also GMT-Documentation). No data allocation, no file opening. 
<newline> (Note pixel registration is converted to grid registration by
modification  
of <tt/xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax/.)

<sect2>gmt_openrasterfile, gmt_closerasterfile
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int  gmt_openrasterfile(struct Grid *g)
void gmt_closerasterfile(struct Grid *g)
</verb></tscreen> opening/closing rasterfile
<p> 

<sect2>gmt_readgrid
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
gmt_readgrid(struct Grid *g)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>get memory for data and read whole raster data from file 


<sect2>gmt_readsubgrid
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Grid * gmt_readsubgrid (struct Grid *oldgrid, 
                               double xmin, double xmax, double ymin, double ymax)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> initialize subgrid and read data from file or copy from old grid, if already read.

<sect2>gmt_writegrid
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int gmt_writegrid (const char *grdfn, struct Grid *g,
		   const char *unit, const int verbose)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>This function writes GMT grid to file (uses xyz2grd of GMT).

<sect2>Examples
<p> Example in which data remains external (not read into internal memory).
<tscreen><verb>
struct Grid *g;
{
   if ((g = gmt_rasterinfo(58) == NULL) return -1;
   gmt_openrasterfile(g);          # just open the file
        ...
   g_intersimple(g, 8, 45, z);     # routine reads the 4 required data points from file
         ...
   gmt_closerasterfile(g);         # close file 
   g_free(g);
}
</verb></tscreen>

<p> Example in which data are read into the internal memory.
<tscreen><verb>
struct Grid *g;
{
   if ((g = gmt_rasterinfo(58) == NULL) return -1;
   gmt_readgrid(g);               # open file, reads entire grid, close file
       ...
   g_intersimple(g, 8, 45, z);    # routine takes the 4 required values from grid
         ...
   g_free(g);
}
</verb></tscreen>

###### SURFER operations ######
<sect1>Operations on Surfer gridfiles
<p>

<sect2>surf_readgrid
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Grid *surf_readgrid(const char *grdfname)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> read surfer grid file ASCII. Grid is initialized in routine, Data buffer
is initialized and read. 


<sect2>surf_writegrid
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int surf_writegrid(const char *grdfname, struct Grid *g, const char *format)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>write surfer grid to file ASCII (example of <tt/format/: "10.3lf") 


